I would rank rows inside each customer partition, ordering by payment time and then a unique payment ID so a tie cannot produce an arbitrary winner. The outer query keeps rank one:

SELECT customer_id, payment_id, paid_at, amount
FROM (
  SELECT p.*, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (
    PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY paid_at DESC, payment_id DESC
  ) AS rn
  FROM payment p
) ranked
WHERE rn = 1;

This returns one row per partition, but it does not mean the greatest ID is semantically newer; that tie-break rule must be approved. If all tied latest payments are required, use a rank based only on timestamp and retain rank one. Decide how null timestamps behave, filter eligible statuses at the intended stage, and test empty customers and timestamp ties. A separate customers join is required if customers without payments must appear.
